---
// The questions: five, by value, the first open; the accordion is questions.js. The cost answer says
// what the code holds: no API key, and a wait for the plan's limit to reset; Lookover can't stop a plan's own paid
// extra usage, so it says to leave that off, as the terms page does.
import { DOCS } from "../../landing/facts.ts";
---

<section class="faq" id="faq">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="slab">
      <p class="label">Questions</p>
      <h2 class="display">Before you run it</h2>
    </div>
    <details open>
      <summary>What does it cost?</summary>
      <div class="panel">
        <p>
          Nothing beyond the Claude or ChatGPT plan you have. A review draws on your plan's limits the way a session in
          the terminal would. Lookover reviews one pull request at a time per AI, uses no API key, and waits for your
          plan's limit to reset rather than paying past it. If your plan can buy extra usage, <a href={DOCS.extraUsage}
            >leave that off</a
          >.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Which plans work?</summary>
      <div class="panel">
        <p>
          Claude Pro or Max, through the <code>claude</code> CLI; ChatGPT Plus or Pro, through the <code>codex</code> CLI.
          One is enough. With both, the AI that did not write the code looks first and the other double-checks.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Does my code leave my machine?</summary>
      <div class="panel">
        <p>
          Only where it already goes: to GitHub, and to Claude or ChatGPT through their own CLIs. Nothing to us. No
          account, no telemetry; the source is open, so you can check. <a href={DOCS.security}
            >Where your code goes, in the docs.</a
          >
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>What does it post on GitHub?</summary>
      <div class="panel">
        <p>
          One review in a few words with the findings on the lines they are about, one summary comment that stays up to
          date, and a check on the commit. An empty review is one line. It never merges, approves or requests changes.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Is this allowed by Claude's and ChatGPT's terms?</summary>
      <div class="panel">
        <p>
          Lookover only runs the official <code>claude</code> and <code>codex</code> CLIs, signed in through their
          own flows, on your machine, for your own pull requests. It never reads or stores your tokens. Your plan's terms
          apply: one subscription, one person, so each colleague runs their own. Lookover is not affiliated with Anthropic
          or OpenAI. <a href={DOCS.terms}>Your plan's terms, in the docs.</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>
